{
 "id": 164,
 "created_at": "2026-10-27T20:25:00.264624+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcMmUb1x9Pk/",
 "title": "The One Rule of Resigning",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator shares a personal story from 2001 when he resigned from Galleon Group and turned down a $300,000 counter-offer to stay. His uncle's advice was that employers never forget the day you took money out of their pockets, leading to his rule: if you plan to resign, be prepared to leave.",
 "key_points": [
  "In 2001, the creator was 31 years old, making $150,000 a year at the Galleon Group, and planned to leave to start his own hedge fund called Argus Partners.",
  "His uncle, an industry veteran, warned him that if he went in to resign, he had to be prepared to leave because companies never forget the day you took money out of their pockets.",
  "When he resigned in a meeting with Raj Rajaratnam (head of Galleon) and Gary (head of the trading desk), they offered to wire $300,000 right then to his bank account if he stayed.",
  "He turned down the money and left.",
  "A friend who also tried to resign from the same firm but decided to stay ended up getting a donut at bonus time.",
  "The one rule of resigning: be prepared to leave."
 ],
 "content": "This is what happened when they dangled $300,000 in front of my face #resign #wallstreet #money #nyc [unclear] If you ever plan on leaving your job or resigning, you need to hear this. I once was offered $300,000 to stay on the spot, and this is what happened. I was making $150,000 a year at the Galleon Group. It is 2001 and I get an opportunity to start my own hedge fund called Argus Partners. [unclear] definite, definite risks to leaving, but it was exciting, I was 31 years old and I was ready to take my shot. So I spoke to my uncle who was an industry vet and he said to me, he said, Tuney, he said, if you go in there to resign, you better be prepared to leave. And I was like, okay, but why? And he said, look, he said, I don't care how much money they offer you, they will never forget the day you took money out of their pockets. And so I was like, oh god, oh god. So I go in there, and I go into a conference room with Raj Rajaratnam, the head of Galleon, and Gary, the head of the trading desk. And we close the conference room door and I sit down and I resign, you know, politely and respectfully and I thank them for the opportunity and they look at each other and they say, we will wire you $300,000 right now to your bank account before we leave this room, if you stay. And I was like, thank you, but uh I've made up my mind, I'm I'm going to resign. So I left $300,000 on the table and sure enough, a friend of mine, who had also tried to resign from the same firm, but he stayed, he got a donut at bonus time. And I don't mean the kind from Duncan. So the one rule of resigning, be prepared to leave.",
